/**
@page ownership_and_async Ownership and asynchronous work

@section ownership_basics References and borrowed values

A borrowed pointer does not carry a reference for the recipient. Acquire a
reference before storing it beyond the owner's lifetime; do not put a borrowed
value directly into an automatic cleanup owner. An owned return value needs the
matching release operation, even when a later step fails.

| API | Ownership |
| --- | --- |
| FileDataRef construction/reset | Adds a reference to the supplied FileData; does not adopt an existing reference. |
| FileDataRef pointer access | Borrowed pointer; no reference is added. |
| FileDataRef::release() | Transfers the held reference and empties the owner. |
| image_loader_new() | Owned GObject reference; the loader also references the source FileData. |
| image_loader_get_fd() / image_loader_get_pixbuf() | Borrowed source/pixels. Reference them before retaining them independently. |
| image_loader_dup_error() | Owned GError copy; use g_error_free(). |
| pixbuf_renderer_get_pixbuf() | Borrowed pixels retained by the renderer. |
| renderer_tiles_surface_to_texture() | Owned immutable GdkTexture copy; use g_object_unref(). |
| FileUtilDoneFunc path / FileTreeDone error | Borrowed only during that callback. |

A new FileData already carries a reference. Constructing a FileDataRef from it
adds another: the original reference still needs releasing. Moves transfer the
held reference. Before move-assigning to a nonempty FileDataRef, reset that
owner to nullptr: the current move-assignment implementation does not release
its previous reference.

References preserve object lifetime, not thread safety or immutability. A
partially decoded pixbuf can still change while another owner holds a reference.
FileData references do not keep image windows or views alive.

@section ownership_loader Image-loader lifecycle

<ol>
<li>Create an ImageLoader, retain its reference, and connect signals on the GTK
main thread before calling image_loader_start() or image_loader_start_deferred().</li>
<li>Decoding normally runs on a worker thread. Progress, size, area, completion,
and error signals are delivered through the default main context. Signal
rectangle/size pointers are valid only during the emission.</li>
<li>A successful start means work was submitted, not that decoding succeeded.
Use the completion/error signals rather than the presence of a pixbuf to
determine the outcome. Keep independent references to pixels needed afterward.</li>
<li>To abandon a loader, release its last reference. Finalization removes pending
starts/signals, requests cancellation, and waits for active decoding to stop.
A blocking decoder can delay this step. No final signal is promised after
cancellation by finalization.</li>
</ol>

Disabling image_loader_delay_area_ready() emits queued area signals synchronously
before returning. Keep both the loader and callback context valid throughout that
call, and account for reentrancy.

ImageWindow cleanup releases its active and read-ahead loaders. If another owner
keeps a loader alive, disconnect handlers whose user data belongs to the closing
window; the loader does not own that callback context. Backend implementations
report through the loader and must not call GTK from decoding callbacks.

@section ownership_renderer Renderer callbacks

PixbufRenderer owns its RendererFuncs backends and source pixbuf reference.
Rendering, tile requests, and post-processing run on the GTK main thread, often
from queued idle work. Borrowed callback arguments must not be retained without
acquiring the appropriate ownership.

PostProcessFunc and tile callbacks are copied into renderer state. Their captures
must remain valid until the callbacks are cleared/replaced and pending users have
been accounted for. Renderer copy/move operations can propagate callbacks to
another renderer. A raw ImageWindow pointer in a lambda is not kept alive by the
lambda. Clear or rebind such callbacks before releasing their context.

Post-processing receives the address of a renderer-owned pixbuf reference. If it
replaces the pixbuf, it must release the old reference and provide an owned
replacement. Tile-request callbacks fill renderer-owned pixels; tile-disposal
callbacks borrow those pixels and must not unref them. Disposal is an eviction
notification, not guaranteed notification for every tile freed at shutdown.

Backend teardown removes pending render sources. GTK snapshots can still retain
immutable textures/render nodes independently of that backend; mutable Cairo/tile
storage must not be exported as if it were immutable.

@section ownership_operations File operations and window closure

The list-based operation entry points in @ref utilops.h consume the list and its
FileData references, including setup failures. Copy the list with
filelist_copy() if the caller needs to keep it. Separate source FileData
arguments are borrowed and referenced internally when retained.

User-facing operations run on the main thread but can open dialogs and finish
later. FileUtilDoneFunc can also run before the initiating function returns.
Its boolean describes the operation workflow, not a universal all-files-succeeded
result: Discard is a non-cancelled terminal phase. Setup failures and a cancelled
file_util_create_dir() chooser can produce no callback at all.

file_tree_task_run() has a different contract: work runs on a worker and done runs
in the creating main context after work returns. Closing its progress dialog
requests cooperative cancellation and completion is still delivered. Read-only
scans may discard their results after cancellation; mutating operations preserve
the actual outcome, including a successful move completed just before cancellation.
Cancelled or failed operations may already have changed the filesystem.

A parent window is presentation context, not an automatic task cancellation
mechanism. When capturing UI state for completion, use an appropriate weak
reference, a live-owner lookup, or separately owned state. Holding a widget
reference does not preserve a C++ view structure destroyed by a widget's destroy
handler. Revalidate the owner before updating its widgets or selections.
*/
